#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185041} :activity/id #uuid "5c37473f-3f15-50ad-88ea-ddb970802b55" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050756 :topic/id #uuid "642d830f-d131-58c7-ae56-060749a40776" :topic/math-academy-id 3811} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185041 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185359 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will learn how to deal with limits involving inverse trigonometric functions. \n\nLet's begin by recalling the graph of $y=\\arcsin(x).$\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nUsing the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The domain of $\\arcsin(x)$ is $x \\in [-1,1].$\n\n- For every value $a \\in (-1,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arcsin(x) = \\arcsin(a).\n$$\n\n \nIn other words, we can evaluate the limit using direct substitution. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1/2}\\operatorname{arcsin}(x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For any value $a$ that does *not* lie in the domain of $\\arcsin(x),$ the limit\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arcsin(x)\n$$\n\n \ndoes *not* exist.\n\n- At the point $x=-1,$ the limit does *not* exist because the left-sided limit does not exist. However, the right-sided limit *does* exist and is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\operatorname{arcsin}(x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}(-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, at the point $x=1,$ the limit does *not* exist because the right-sided limit does not exist. However, the left-sided limit *does* exist and is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\operatorname{arcsin}(x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit properties of $y=\\arccos(x)$ are similar. Let's discuss them briefly." :tutorial/id #uuid "f1bc1975-006b-5be4-ad5c-88888abb00d5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6599 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "003ba853-1e82-584c-b1f2-becf3ff9c30c" :step/math-academy-id 39810 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185052}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185042 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184238 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183921 :question/id #uuid "563087c2-8d87-5b95-833d-f9892d9276a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-10535"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f458c61-bc51-5653-9ecb-1e96eb5c335e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050071}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314754}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Limit of an Inverse Trigonometric Function at a Point"} :step/id #uuid "e4ed7c3e-7162-558c-a918-39f63652e0c0" :step/math-academy-id 39811 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185044}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185043 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184239 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183922 :question/id #uuid "c1ba0b9f-78d6-5791-8816-b71da089e57f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10536"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bcc5d54-e7fd-5be5-9d00-4240d03e21cb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050085}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184517} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317370}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Limits at Infinity"} :step/id #uuid "9666a609-3291-5b2c-a5f1-4c905924ddd1" :step/math-academy-id 39814 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185045}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185044 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185360 :tutorial/content "We can deduce limits at infinity of transformed inverse trigonometric functions by sketching their graphs.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\dfrac{2}{\\pi}\\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right)\n$$\n\nTo evaluate this limit, we first sketch the graph of  $y =\\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right){:}$ \n\n![](images/51/51a0b9a210147b3791ce20a2f65320e20618826671ea6c7c31a27f8647595079.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right)$ is $(-\\infty,\\infty).$   Moreover, from the graph, we see that \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to \\infty} \\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right)=\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} }  .\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the algebra of limits, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}(x-\\pi ) & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}(x-\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLimits at infinity involving the inverse sine and cosine functions usually do not exist. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "24cdcba9-d6c5-5719-a086-34fe417cc42f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6600 :tutorial/title "Limits of Inverse Trigonometric Function at Infinity"} :step/id #uuid "66882814-9d8d-500a-b199-d60870f60cec" :step/math-academy-id 39815 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185043}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185045 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184240 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183923 :question/id #uuid "7ce46ca9-cfff-5e8b-89b9-a2a7b34921fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-10537"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a58f115-f368-5171-af73-37084085b42a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048465}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317512}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Limit Involving Trigonometric Functions and Their Inverses"} :step/id #uuid "f8205256-4d92-59b5-babd-ec4ae58b06b8" :step/math-academy-id 39823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185051 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185362 :tutorial/content "The graph of $y=\\arctan x$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc06a4e63f38a315dd3944f65f871cca2dbdddee26ec268e857f79e5f4ab8848.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The domain is $x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\n- For every value $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arctan(x) = \\arctan(a).\n$$\n\n- The limit as $x\\to-\\infty$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}(x) & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The limit as $x\\to\\infty$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}(x) & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the algebra of limits to evaluate expressions involving inverse trigonometric functions. Let's see some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "926b00b5-d0b1-559a-9b04-c0c0636824d6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6741 :tutorial/title "Limits of Inverse Tangent"} :step/id #uuid "338537ec-9b29-534a-bc92-7f0375a54a6d" :step/math-academy-id 40843 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185042}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185052 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185363 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\arccos(x).$\n\n![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The domain is $x \\in [-1,1].$\n\n- For every value $a \\in (-1,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arccos(x) = \\arccos(a).\n$$\n\n- For any value $a$ that does *not* lie in the domain of $\\arccos(x),$ the limit\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arccos(x)\n$$\n\n \ndoes *not* exist.\n\n- The right-sided limit at $x=-1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\operatorname{arccos}(x) & =\\operatorname{arccos}(-1) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the left-sided limit at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\operatorname{arccos}(x) & =\\operatorname{arccos}(1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's deduce the limit properties of $y = \\arctan x.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "eea2d2ac-ea9c-551a-91c6-4ee85ab05907" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6742 :tutorial/title "Limits of Inverse Cosine"} :step/id #uuid "13c5d606-b95e-5a64-ba04-2a68fbf50e14" :step/math-academy-id 40844 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185051}}] :activity/title "Limits of Inverse Trigonometric Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186184398}]}
